WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.200
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:13.200
Finally, today, DeepMind’s amazing AI,&nbsp;
MuZero that plays Chess and other games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:20.400
has now finally entered the real world and has&nbsp;
learned to solve important real-world problems.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:29.600
This is a reinforcement learning technique that&nbsp;
works really well on games. Why? Well, in Chess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:37.600
Go and Starcraft, the controls are clear, we use&nbsp;
the mouse to move our units around or choose where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:45.840
to move our pieces. And the score is also quite&nbsp;
clear: we get rewarded if we win the game. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:53.280
is going to be our score. To say that these worked&nbsp;
really well would be an understatement: DeepMind’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:02.240
MuZero is one of the best in the world in Chess,&nbsp;
Go, and Stacraft 2 as well. But one important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:09.600
question still remains. Of course, they did not&nbsp;
create this AI to play video games. They created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:18.080
it to be able to become a general-purpose AI&nbsp;
that can solve not just games, but many problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:24.560
The games are just used as an excellent&nbsp;
testbed for this AI. So, what else can it do?

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:32.720
Well, finally here it is! Hold on to your papers,&nbsp;
because scientists at DeepMind decided to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:40.640
using their MuZero AI to create a real solution&nbsp;
to a very important problem. Video compression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:48.240
And here comes the twist - they said, let’s&nbsp;
imagine that video compression is a video game.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:57.440
Okay, that’s crazy, but let’s accept it for now.&nbsp;
But then, two questions: what are the controls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:02.640
and what is the score? How do we&nbsp;
know if we won video compression?

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:09.760
Well, the video game controller in our hand will&nbsp;
be is choosing the parameters of the video encoder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:16.720
for each frame. Okay, but there needs to&nbsp;
be a score. So what is the score here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:24.320
How do we win? Well, we win if we are able to&nbsp;
choose the parameters such that the quality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:32.000
the output video is as good as with the previous&nbsp;
compression algorithms, but, the size of the video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:38.480
is smaller. The smaller the output video,&nbsp;
the better. That is going to be our score.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:46.000
And, it also uses self-competition, which&nbsp;
is now a popular concept in video game AIs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.360
This means that the AI plays against previous&nbsp;
versions of itself, and we measure its improvement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:59.360
by it being able to defeat these previous&nbsp;
versions. If it can reliably do that, we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:08.160
can conclude that the AI is indeed improving. This&nbsp;
concept works on boxing, playing catch, Starcraft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:12.960
and I wonder how this would&nbsp;
work for video compression?

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:18.080
Well, let’s see. Let’s immediately&nbsp;
drop it into deep waters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:25.040
Yes, we are going to test this against a&nbsp;
mature, state of art video compression algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:32.320
that you are likely already using at this very&nbsp;
moment as you are watching this on Youtube. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:39.360
good luck little AI, but I’ll be honest, there&nbsp;
is not much hope here. These traditional video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.720
compression algorithms are a culmination&nbsp;
of decades of ingenious human research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:52.240
Can a newcomer AI beat it? I am not&nbsp;
sure. And now, hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.240 --> 00:04:00.560
and let’s see together. How did it go? So, a 4%&nbsp;
difference. So, a learning-based algorithm that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:10.240
is just 4% worse than decades of human innovation?&nbsp;
That is great! But…wait a second, it's actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:22.080
not worse. Can it be that…yes! It is not 4% worse.&nbsp;
It is even 4% better. Holy mother of papers, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:30.160
is absolutely incredible. Yes, this is the corner&nbsp;
of the internet where we get super excited by a 4%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:37.920
better solution, and understand why that matters&nbsp;
a great deal. Welcome to Two Minute Papers!

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.960
But wait, we are experienced Fellow Scholars&nbsp;
over here, we know that it is very easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:51.520
be better by 4% in size at the cost of decreased&nbsp;
quality. But having the same quality and save 4%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.240 --> 00:05:00.160
is insanely difficult. So, which one is it? Let’s&nbsp;
look together. I am flicking between the state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:08.000
of the art and the new technique, and, yes, my&nbsp;
goodness, the results really speak for themselves.

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:16.320
So, let’s look a bit under the hood and see&nbsp;
some more about the decisions the AI is making.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:26.240
Whoa. That is really cool. So what is this? Here,&nbsp;
we see the scores for the previous technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:33.440
and the new AI, and here, they appear to be&nbsp;
making similar decisions on this cover song video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:40.240
but the AI makes somewhat better&nbsp;
decisions overall. That is very cool.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:47.920
But, look at that! In the second half&nbsp;
of this gaming video, MuZero makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.960
vastly different, and, vastly&nbsp;
better decisions. I love it.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:59.520
And to have a first crack at such a mature&nbsp;
problem, and manage to improve it immediately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.800
that is almost completely unheard of. Yet, they&nbsp;
have done it with protein folding, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.280
they seem to have done it for video&nbsp;
compression too. Bravo, DeepMind!

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:18.160
And note the meaning in the magnitude of the&nbsp;
difference here. For instance, OpenAI’s Dall-E 2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:24.400
was this much better than Dall-E 1. That’s&nbsp;
not 4% better, if that was a percentage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:30.640
this would be several hundred percent&nbsp;
better. So, why get so excited about 4%?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:38.948
Well, the key is that 3-4% more compression is&nbsp;
incredible given how well polished these state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.948 --> 00:06:44.720
of the art techniques are. VP9 compressors&nbsp;
are not some first crack at the problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:49.120
no-no. This is a mature field&nbsp;
with decades of experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:56.800
where every percent of improvement requires blood,&nbsp;
papers and tears, and of course, lots of compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:02.960
and memory. And this is just the first crack&nbsp;
at the problem for DeepMind, and we get not 1%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:13.360
but 4% essentially for free. That is absolutely&nbsp;
amazing. My mind is blown by this result. Wow.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:19.760
And, I also wanted to thank you for watching&nbsp;
this video. I truly love talking about these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:25.600
amazing research papers, and I am really&nbsp;
